# The transport suite's declared host settings: vm.sh transport-tune applies
# them and records what each host then has (bench/transport-tune.txt), and
# fairness.py compares that record with this file. One setting for every arm:
# no arm gets a value a comparable arm was not swept for, but where a knob's
# best differs by technology (TR_BEST_BY_TECHNOLOGY: each arm at its own).
# Sourced by vm.sh.

# CPU map (host.sh transport_cpu_map): SMT off, the first core housekeeping,
# the second every data-NIC IRQ, the rest isolated (isolcpus, nohz_full,
# rcu_nocbs; irqaffinity the housekeeping CPU). A session's SMT is its sets
# file's (`smt on`: kept on, each isolated core whole in the map;
# transport-cli.md), never this file's: one source for it

# kernel data path for every socket and kernel-UDP arm (busy poll, us).
# TR_BUSY_READ is also every socket arm's own SO_BUSY_POLL (udp_lat/tcp_lat
# --busy-read-us, the per-arm knob busy_read_us below), mio's and
# ergon-io's alike (epoll-spin's busy_poll_us key)
TR_BUSY_READ=50
TR_BUSY_POLL=50
# NAPI on the data NICs' VFs: the hosts' own (vm.sh transport-net, put back
# after each socket arm), as kernel-busy's media driver has it. Not every
# arm's: the per-arm knob napi_defer below gives each technology its own
TR_NAPI_DEFER=2
TR_GRO_FLUSH_NS=200000

# TCP (every TCP arm): Nagle off and quick ACKs are the binaries' socket
# options (transport-cli.md); these are the host's
TR_TCP_CONGESTION=cubic
TR_TCP_SACK=1
TR_TCP_TIMESTAMPS=1
TR_TCP_SLOW_START_AFTER_IDLE=0
TR_TCP_RMEM="4096 1048576 16777216"
TR_TCP_WMEM="4096 1048576 16777216"
TR_CORE_RMEM_MAX=16777216
TR_CORE_WMEM_MAX=16777216
# kernel 6.15 and up only (older kernels lack the sysctls: recorded as such).
# The TCP arms' --rto-min-us and --delack-max-us (socket options) go only
# where both hosts' kernels have the sysctl (transport.sh tr_timer_args): the
# binaries refuse a timer the kernel lacks or would round. Delayed-ACK max
# 8000 us: two ticks at CONFIG_HZ=250 (Debian's), the kernel's least there,
# and valid at HZ=1000 too
TR_TCP_RTO_MIN_US=8000
TR_TCP_DELACK_MAX_US=8000
# every data-subnet route, in every table, with quickack 1
TR_QUICKACK_ROUTES=1

# data NICs (the VFs): interrupt coalescing off, rings
TR_COALESCE_OFF=1
TR_RX_RING=1024
TR_TX_RING=1024

# Aeron, every Aeron arm (aeron_lat's driver): DEDICATED, busy-spin, the
# conductor on a core of its own, 1 MiB terms (the /dev/shm budget at 128
# streams), no sparse files, MTU 1408
TR_AERON_MODE_SUFFIX=@CONDUCTOR=own@AERON_TERM_BUFFER_LENGTH=1048576@AERON_IPC_TERM_BUFFER_LENGTH=1048576@AERON_CONDUCTOR_IDLE_STRATEGY=spin@AERON_SENDER_IDLE_STRATEGY=noop@AERON_RECEIVER_IDLE_STRATEGY=noop

# aeron_lat's own flags, and the external driver's AERON_DIR (transport.sh
# hands it to vm.sh): tmpfs, Aeron's own place on Linux and memory-backed (T4);
# 8 GiB on the lab's VMs, room for 128 streams' logs on each host
TR_AERON_DIR=/dev/shm/aeron-bench
# an arm at aeron_page=2097152 (aeron-ipc-huge): its driver's and binary's
# AERON_DIR on a hugetlbfs of 2 MiB pages, mode 1777 as /dev/shm, that
# vm.sh transport-tune mounts on each host with its pages reserved
# (min_size), refusing when the kernel has fewer free (bootstrap reserves
# 2048), and verifies (host.sh transport_huge_mount). 32 pages: twice
# what the arm holds at once at 1 MiB terms and 1 stream (transport_huge_pages:
# CnC, loss report, 4 logs; transport.sh check refuses fewer)
TR_AERON_HUGE_MOUNT=/mnt/huge-aeron
TR_AERON_HUGE_DIR=/mnt/huge-aeron/aeron-bench
TR_AERON_HUGE_PAGES=32
TR_TERM_LENGTH=1048576
TR_MTU=1408
# udp_lat and tcp_lat: socket buffers (bytes); mpsc_lat: sync_channel capacity
TR_SOCKBUF=2097152
TR_MPSC_CAPACITY=4096

# every binary: message size (bytes, the same in every arm), base port
# (stream i on base + i), the spike and stall thresholds it stamps (ns)
TR_PAYLOAD=64
# stream i on TR_PORT_BASE + i, up to TR_PORT_SPAN ports: outside the media
# driver's wildcard ranges (20000-21999, vm.sh common_env) and the kernel's
# ephemeral range (host.sh transport_ports_check, at transport-tune)
TR_PORT_BASE=22000
TR_PORT_SPAN=1024
# aeron_lat --driver-busy-threads: the driver's busy threads as vm.sh starts
# it (DEDICATED: sender, receiver, and the conductor on its own core)
TR_AERON_DRIVER_BUSY_THREADS=3
TR_SPIKE_NS=100000
TR_STALL_NS=10000

# ---- Per-arm knobs: each arm runs these unless the tuned section (at the
# end, LAB_TUNE=1) froze another value for it (TR_ARM_KNOBS
# "<arm>:<knob>=<value> ..."; transport.sh tr_knob). Knob, its default, and
# how the runner applies it (transport.sh tr_bin_args, tr_driver_mode):
#   busy_read_us      TR_BUSY_READ        udp_lat/tcp_lat --busy-read-us (mio and ergon-io)
#   epoll_events      TR_EPOLL_EVENTS     udp_lat/tcp_lat --epoll-events (mio and ergon-io)
#   datagram_burst    TR_DATAGRAM_BURST   udp_lat --datagram-burst (ergon-io only)
#   sockbuf           TR_SOCKBUF          udp_lat/tcp_lat --rcvbuf and --sndbuf
#   quickack_per_read TR_QUICKACK_PER_READ  tcp_lat --quickack-per-read [false]
#   rto_min_us        TR_TCP_RTO_MIN_US   tcp_lat --rto-min-us, where both kernels have it
#   congestion        TR_TCP_CONGESTION   the hosts' tcp_congestion_control (one value for
#                                         every TCP arm: the host's, not a socket's)
#   term_length       TR_TERM_LENGTH      aeron_lat --term-length (every Aeron arm)
#   aeron_path        TR_AERON_PATH       the kernel arms' media driver: kernel-busy or kernel
#   aeron_so_rcvbuf   TR_AERON_SO_RCVBUF  the kernel arms' driver AERON_SOCKET_SO_RCVBUF
#   nak_delay_ns      TR_AERON_NAK_DELAY_NS  the driver's AERON_NAK_UNICAST_DELAY (ns)
#   rx_free_thresh    TR_DPDK_RX_FREE_THRESH  the DPDK driver's RUSTERON_DPDK_RX_FREE_THRESH
#                                         (pmd: unset, the PMD's own)
#   aeron_threading   TR_AERON_THREADING  an Aeron arm's driver's threading (aeron-ipc's, and
#                                         every --mode udp arm's on a driver): DEDICATED,
#                                         SHARED_NETWORK (sender and receiver one agent,
#                                         the conductor on its own core), SHARED (one agent
#                                         for all) or INVOKER (aeron_driver embedded alone:
#                                         the binary's measuring thread runs the driver);
#                                         its busy threads 3, 2, 1, 0 (tr_driver_busy)
#   aeron_driver      TR_AERON_DRIVER     every --mode udp Aeron arm's on a driver: external
#                                         (vm.sh's media driver process) or embedded (no such
#                                         process: aeron_lat --driver embedded, or invoker,
#                                         starts it with ~/bench/driver.env, the same
#                                         settings, under fstack_lat's privileges)
#   capacity          TR_MPSC_CAPACITY    mpsc_lat --capacity
#   aeron_page        TR_AERON_PAGE       every Aeron arm's AERON_DIR (driver and aeron_lat
#                                         --aeron-dir): 4096 TR_AERON_DIR (tmpfs), 2097152
#                                         TR_AERON_HUGE_DIR (2 MiB hugetlbfs); read back
#                                         per point (vm.sh aeron-page: statfs's block size)
#   napi_defer        TR_SOCKET_NAPI_DEFER  every data NIC's napi_defer_hard_irqs (0: and
#                                         gro_flush_timeout 0; n: TR_GRO_FLUSH_NS): a socket
#                                         arm's (setup sockets, an arms file's too) by
#                                         transport-net's TR_OVERRIDES, the hosts' put back
#                                         after; an Aeron kernel UDP arm's is its driver's
#                                         (aeron_path kernel-busy: TR_AERON_NAPI_DEFER; kernel: 0);
#                                         an Onload arm's TR_ONLOAD_NAPI_DEFER (vm.sh onload-up).
#                                         Read back per point (vm.sh napi)
#   irq_idle          TR_IRQ_IDLE         the IRQ core's idle depth (the map's IRQ CPUs):
#                                         all (no cap), or n (every cpuidle state
#                                         deeper than the n-th disabled there: 1 the
#                                         first halt state, 0 poll only): a socket
#                                         arm's by transport-net's TR_OVERRIDES, all
#                                         put back at the next arm's. Read back per
#                                         point (vm.sh idle: each housekeeping and IRQ
#                                         CPU's states, their usage and time, before
#                                         and after)
TR_EPOLL_EVENTS=64
TR_DATAGRAM_BURST=64
TR_QUICKACK_PER_READ=on
TR_AERON_PATH=kernel-busy
TR_AERON_SO_RCVBUF=2097152
# Aeron 1.53.3's C driver's own: 1 us
TR_AERON_NAK_DELAY_NS=1000
TR_DPDK_RX_FREE_THRESH=pmd
TR_AERON_THREADING=DEDICATED
TR_AERON_DRIVER=external
TR_AERON_PAGE=4096
# The socket arms' data NICs interrupt at once: they wait in epoll with a
# zero timeout, which never busy-polls the NIC queue, so with interrupts
# deferred every received packet waits for the gro_flush timer on each host.
# The a2 session (20261026, napi_defer 2, gro_flush 200 us for every arm):
# the socket arms' p50 ~290 us against a min of 60-80 us, and ergon-io's N2
# check warned "interrupts are deferred ... packets wait for the 200 us
# timer" on every socket point (a binary's warning breaks fairness.py's
# hostcheck rule). Aeron's kernel arms: TR_AERON_NAPI_DEFER below
TR_SOCKET_NAPI_DEFER=0
# The Aeron kernel UDP arms' deferral (their driver's @NAPI_DEFER): 0 too.
# kernel-busy's 2 / 200 us suits one busy-polled socket, but with several
# channels a packet lands on a queue the receiver is not polling then and
# waits for the timer: session a1' (20261026-132809, set 2a, 50k msg/s)
# aeron-udp p50 58 us at 1 stream, 269-296 us at 8, 32 and 128 streams
# (DPDK 84-98 us across them). Busy polling stays on (net.core.busy_read,
# busy_poll 50 us): only the interrupt deferral is off, as for the sockets
TR_AERON_NAPI_DEFER=0
# The IRQ core's idle states: every one the cpuidle driver offers (all). The
# c7i sessions' 10k msg/s socket points (aws-20261028-071640) took their
# packets in the IRQ core's softirq after a wake from idle; mio-tcp-c1 and
# mio-tcp-c0 cap it (TR_DECLARED)
TR_IRQ_IDLE=all
# Knobs whose best differs by technology, each arm at its own: fairness.py's
# shared-knob rule takes an arm at its technology's best (transport_collect.py
# best.knob.<name>: a socket arm at TR_SOCKET_NAPI_DEFER, an Aeron kernel UDP
# arm at its driver's) as needing no sweep; an arm off it still needs one,
# and fairness.py's napi rule refuses a socket point whose NICs deferred
# unless a tuning session froze that for its arm
TR_BEST_BY_TECHNOLOGY="napi_defer"

# Declared technology differences: "<arm>:<twin>:<knob>=<value> ...", an arm
# that is its twin's arm row and runs every other knob at the twin's value
# (the tuned section's for the twin too), this one knob apart: the
# difference the pair measures. transport.sh check refuses an arm row that
# is not the twin's; fairness.py's declared rule checks the rest per round.
# The hand-off arms (transport-aws-aeron-handoff.tsv): dpdk-aeron-rel with
# its driver in the binary's process (-emb), sender and receiver one thread
# (-shnet), everything one thread (-shared), and the driver run by the
# binary's measuring thread (-invoker: -emb's twin, so INVOKER on top of
# embedded); aeron-rel's -emb and -invoker the same on kernel UDP. The c7i
# diagnosis (transport-aws-c7i-diag.tsv): mio-tcp with no SO_BUSY_POLL
# (-br0: interrupts alone), and its IRQ core held to the first halt state
# (-c1) or to polling (-c0)
TR_DECLARED="mio-tcp-br0:mio-tcp:busy_read_us=0 mio-tcp-c1:mio-tcp:irq_idle=1 mio-tcp-c0:mio-tcp:irq_idle=0 aeron-ipc-huge:aeron-ipc:aeron_page=2097152 dpdk-aeron-rel-emb:dpdk-aeron-rel:aeron_driver=embedded dpdk-aeron-rel-shnet:dpdk-aeron-rel:aeron_threading=SHARED_NETWORK dpdk-aeron-rel-shared:dpdk-aeron-rel:aeron_threading=SHARED dpdk-aeron-rel-invoker:dpdk-aeron-rel-emb:aeron_threading=INVOKER aeron-rel-emb:aeron-rel:aeron_driver=embedded aeron-rel-invoker:aeron-rel-emb:aeron_threading=INVOKER"

# The tuning session's sweeps (LAB_TUNE=1: transport_tune.py sweep):
# TR_SWEEP_<KNOB>="<arms> <values> <profile>", the knob's default swept too
# (first; a tie keeps it). Each arm and knob is one round of its values
# and an A/A of the default, at its set's middle load and at 1 and 128
# streams where its sets have them; profile loss runs them under netem
# TR_TUNE_NETEM % as well (the recovery knobs: no DPDK arm). A knob comparable
# arms share (buffers, epoll events) is swept for each of them.
TR_SWEEP_BUSY_READ_US="mio-udp,eio-udp,mio-tcp,eio-tcp 0,50 load"
TR_SWEEP_NAPI_DEFER="mio-udp,eio-udp,mio-tcp,eio-tcp 0,2 load"
TR_SWEEP_EPOLL_EVENTS="mio-udp,eio-udp,mio-tcp,eio-tcp 64,256 load"
TR_SWEEP_DATAGRAM_BURST="eio-udp 1,16,64 load"
TR_SWEEP_SOCKBUF="mio-udp,eio-udp,mio-tcp,eio-tcp 2097152,4194304 load"
TR_SWEEP_QUICKACK_PER_READ="mio-tcp,eio-tcp off,on load"
TR_SWEEP_RTO_MIN_US="mio-tcp,eio-tcp 8000,12000,16000 loss"
TR_SWEEP_CONGESTION="mio-tcp,eio-tcp cubic,bbr load"
TR_SWEEP_AERON_PATH="aeron-udp,aeron-rel,aeron-self kernel-busy,kernel load"
TR_SWEEP_TERM_LENGTH="aeron-ipc,aeron-spy,aeron-self,dpdk-aeron-self,aeron-udp,dpdk-aeron-udp,aeron-rel,dpdk-aeron-rel 65536,1048576 load"
TR_SWEEP_AERON_SO_RCVBUF="aeron-udp,aeron-rel,aeron-self 2097152,4194304 load"
TR_SWEEP_NAK_DELAY_NS="aeron-rel 1000,20000,100000 loss"
TR_SWEEP_RX_FREE_THRESH="dpdk-aeron-udp,dpdk-aeron-rel,dpdk-aeron-self pmd,32,64 load"
TR_SWEEP_AERON_THREADING="aeron-ipc DEDICATED,SHARED load"
TR_SWEEP_CAPACITY="mpsc 64,1024,4096 load"
TR_TUNE_STREAMS="1 128"
TR_TUNE_NETEM=0.1
TR_TUNE_CONFIRM_REPS=3
# the local smoke's cut-down sweep (transport.sh smoke with LAB_TUNE=1)
TR_SMOKE_TUNE_ARMS="mio-udp,eio-udp,mio-tcp,eio-tcp,aeron-rel,aeron-ipc,mpsc"
TR_SMOKE_TUNE_KNOBS="busy_read_us,datagram_burst,congestion,nak_delay_ns,capacity,aeron_threading"

# ---- Onload (setup onload: transport-aws-onload.tsv, AWS only) ----
# OpenOnload over AF_XDP on the ENA's native XDP, the kernel's own ena driver
# (vm.sh onload-up). The build: Xilinx-CNS/onload at TR_ONLOAD_COMMIT (master,
# 2026-10-23, ONLOAD_VERSION 10.0.0; v9.0.2, the last tag, stops at Linux
# 6.9) with onload-kernel-7.2.patch (Linux 7.2 and AArch64: onload-build.sh),
# AF_XDP only (--no-sfc). Every Onload arm runs the kernel arms' binary under
# `onload --profile=TR_ONLOAD_PROFILE` with TR_ONLOAD_ENV (the media driver
# too, for the Aeron ones), each recorded in its manifest:
#   EF_AF_XDP_ZEROCOPY=0  the in-tree ena has no AF_XDP zero-copy (no
#                         ndo_xsk_wakeup): copy mode, or the stack fails
#   EF_NO_FAIL=0          a socket Onload cannot accelerate fails, never
#                         falls back to the kernel unsaid
#   EF_RFC_RTO_MIN=8      ms: the TCP arms' TR_TCP_RTO_MIN_US (Onload has
#                         no TCP_RTO_MIN_US; TCP_QUICKACK it has)
# No flow filters (enable_af_xdp_flow_filters=0: the ena has no ntuple
# filters), so each data NIC runs one queue (combined 1) and every TCP and
# UDP frame on it goes to the one Onload stack bound there; its MTU at most
# TR_ONLOAD_MTU (ENA_XDP_MAX_MTU at 4 KiB pages); interrupts not deferred
# (TR_ONLOAD_NAPI_DEFER: Onload polls the AF_XDP ring, which the ena's NAPI
# fills on each interrupt, with no busy poll of its own). No socket arm's
# kernel busy poll: --busy-read-us 0, Onload's own spin instead
# (EF_POLL_USEC, the latency profile's)
TR_ONLOAD_REPO=https://github.com/Xilinx-CNS/onload.git
TR_ONLOAD_COMMIT=a945125566261f2f2df9275ae9d2af361265bb65
TR_ONLOAD_PROFILE=latency
TR_ONLOAD_ENV="EF_AF_XDP_ZEROCOPY=0 EF_NO_FAIL=0 EF_RFC_RTO_MIN=8"
TR_ONLOAD_MTU=3498
TR_ONLOAD_NAPI_DEFER=0

# per-point events (vm.sh events-start/stop, transport_events.py): the gap
# spinners' threshold (us) on the hot CPUs a point leaves idle, and the
# trace's buffer a CPU (KiB; a full one is noted as an overrun)
TR_EVENTS_GAPSPIN_US=20
TR_EVENTS_BUFFER_KB=16384

# ==== tuned: generated by transport_tune.py (LAB_TUNE=1); edit by rerunning the tuning, not by hand ====
# No tuning session has run: every arm at the defaults above, no knob swept.
TR_TUNED_RUN=
TR_TUNED_SWEPT=
TR_ARM_KNOBS=
# ==== end tuned ====
